 24 \>>No elastic modulus. No invented filament tension. No microscopic spring constant.
\>>There is a subtle mathematical qualification worth preserving: the second derivative \\(d^2\mathbf x/dw^2\\) is the spatial bending relative to the chosen Newtonian time coordinate. It is not generally identical to the intrinsic curvature of the four-dimensional curve. In the low-velocity limit, however, its magnitude agrees with the leading curvature approximation.


\>>Substructure: H(s)H attempts to explain the observed inertial mass through finer-scale filament morphology and interactions.

Not really, no. SAT began as a way to visualize physics in 4D, no more, no less. But it turned out that when you look at 4D structure, you begin to notice there are much simpler, entirely equivalent ways to describe exactly the same system. Inertia and worldtube bending are equivalent. It's like asking whether the chair is *really* six feet from the east wall or two feet from the south wall. It's both. Just depends where you're measuring from. Of course... in a *true* block universe, there is no force at all. If the past present and future are *purely* locked in with no change, then the time surface is entirely inert with respect to the matter, and it acts exactly like an MRI scanner appears to work--as a readout (this is why I don't like the term "readout" -- it implies there's no interaction, and that *cannot* be the case; at least, it cannot be the case if there's anything at all correct about Newton or Einstein, or basically all of physics; and we see that matter *does* in fact appear to follow invisible forces or metric curvatures, so there's clearly an interaction; time is just moving space).&#x20;

I'm attaching our new standard dimensional normalization here; not sure if you've been using that or not, but I think it's probably our best Lingua Franca. I'm also not sure if it contains the 50:50 H_0+c [yes, I know these have to be converted into the same units; it's just shorthand] split or not... that is, given that, in SAT, our observation that space is expanding combined with the SAT assertion that *time is pushing the excitements we call light* at c... leads us to the conclusion, backed up by the observation that in our model, the simplest (orthogonality) assumption is that the w axis should point outwards from all points, and that is indeed what we see when we observe (mathematically) the expansion of a propagation sphere of emission from an EM source. Eg. Time is pushing light outwards from a lightbulb or a candle or a supernova at exactly c, and the light sphere (again, 1:1 with a light cone, just using other terms) illustrates this precisely. Measurement of light is measurement of time. No surprise there. And it's not a "sheet" of course, it's a 3D "slice", it's a bulk that overlaps with the xyz bulk as wxyz. [Incidentally--or rather, to make a point as it just occurs to me--I once had NotebookLM's deep dive try to pull a "gotcha" by focusing on the "problem" of the light sphere and the differential expansion of space along w as compared to xyz, saying... "if that were the case, then it wouldn't be a *sphere* now would it?" They didn't say it quite so smarmily... but I dismissed it at the time, thinking... well c seems *huge* to humans, but at cosmological scales, or at the scale of light travel time from a lightbulb the difference would be so minute that it wouldn't cause a measurable deviation; and besides, we don't *actually* see a sphere of light expanding from a lightbulb, so any distortion if it were present would have to be measured per variance in the distance traveled by light rays or photons in different orientations, and I doubt that would be even remotely measurable. --But there's an enormous *flaw* in my own thinking there, I eventually realized--but I only just realized the fuller retort. Do you see the basic error I made there?]&#x20;

Anyway, I'm also giving you the recent "how to" written by, I guess, Caliper after our conversation trying to construct superhelices as I imagine them (and again, I can be wrong--but it's dependent upon 1) the frequency of the oscillation we're modeling as the 4D physical correlate of the helical path [the answer has usually been taken to be: spin -- so if spin has a frequency, then we can actually calculate the wrapping over a given distance -- or if it's the case, as I think should at least be our starting assumption, that the apparent motion of particles (and other objects) is exactly c, either inherently, or as I think increasingly seems to make the most sense, as an emergent result of the flexibility of the timesheet/medium, the frictional or inductive or whatever resistance we observe as metric curvature in the timesheet as induced by the presence of matter is basically an inductive drag--that is--the timesheet passes along a worldtube at a more or less fixed rate defined by the resistance that worldtubes push back on it with; since a coiled worldtube covers a much 

 37 No we should absolutely *not* treat them as filament thickness. We should treat them as bounds for the presumed spin-equivalent first order coil diameter. That is, they should be in the neighborhood of the \~0.7 fm *radius* value of the (misnamed) \\(\ell\\)\_f &#x20;

When we're working at this scale, we always need to be clear not to mix up scale order. It's particularly confusing, because the filament *is* what sets the size of its coil, by virtue of its stiffness. But we can determine exact stiffness simply by looking at precision data for the particle's momentum under a known force.&#x20;

So... the worldtube \\(\ell\\) ...which should probably just get a new symbol that stands for diameter, since "length" gives the impression that it's a linear length along the helix. It's axis-to-circumference *length*, yes. But the terminology is irregular for the object under study.&#x20;

And, actually, if you're modeling this as a toroidal winding, then we need the 4D coil that produces it. What you've got in the earlier image I liked is, it appears, a 3D intersectional trace of a 2nd order 4D filament; you don't have a filment itself. With 4D filaments, I don't know that we should even *expect* self-closure in the 3D cobordism. So, to do this SAT-native, we have to start with a 4D filament.

