 47 Cross-posting from Mercer for visibility:

\>>Given SAT's worldlines in Minkowski spacetime, what additional geometry is required to obtain finite-core worldtubes and their interactions?

And the answer to that is--tentatively--worldtubes constructed as ER bridges on the Kerr black hole model, where the ring singularity is the intersectional trace of a tube "singularity", which provides the Fermi exclusion rule. The intersectional trace of the shell structure is likely what String Theory calls a closed string. Its topology likely defines ST vibration modes. That's the working H(s)H hypothesis.&#x20;

You'll need to use the toolkit, and you'll need to make sure you're abreast of all the available conversations currently in the development convos, including those that ended up in the CONVOS outside of the organizing fodlers, and those that ended up in the root. Be sure also that you're regularly reviewing random doc pulls from the old Satobloc archive, and incorporating/testing ideas that have been floated but not followed up on, and so that you've got the chance to unearth some useful equations that have fallen between the cracks. Keep in mind that the formalisms and constructions have changed but that the core "ontology" for lack of a better word has only been slightly modified along the way. The biggest modification, and the first that steps beyond Strict Minkowski Sufficiency > Strict Minkowski Construction + Conjectured Kinematics-Based Substructure (for normally non-kinematic/non-geometric abstractions like spin, flavor, etc) ...is the responsive medium for electromagnetism/electrogravity. That was necessary to explain Coulomb repulsion, because it allowed Kelvin vortices as our primary electrogravity mechanism. Whether constructed as a responsive medium per se, or in the older SAT timesheet soliton/t-boson f-boson vocabulary is somewhat of an open question. Effectively, the split between those two branches boils down to: Do electromagnetic effects or do they not persist in effecting past worldtube geometries in parts of the bulk where the/a timesheet has already/hasn't yet passed, or is electromagnetism a purely "in the moment" force? I suspect the latter might be likelier, but I'm by no means certain about that. Worldtube physical continuity, and the things we've explained as a result of "back-tug" and "transtemporal tug" are enough to establish gravitation and interbraid/strong forces firmly within SAT, and therefore firmly within the H(s)H initial inheritance. What electromagnetism does, if anything, in front of/behind the timesheet is a different question. But we should try to keep the time wavefront thickness as the area of "active update" of the dynamic block as an operational prerogative for now, and see how conservatively SAT we can be before CODATA and the internal logic of "SAT+ER/Kerr" core of H(s)H.&#x20;

